# The OCR model assets are read byte for byte, so git must not touch their line endings.
#
# dict.txt maps one character per line onto the recogniser's output indices. With the usual
# CRLF conversion every entry gains a trailing carriage return, so the model's table no longer
# says what the model was trained to say and recognition comes back as nonsense — the failure
# is silent, survives a clean build, and only shows up as garbled text on screen. Caught when
# PP-OCRv6 landed: the file was written with LF, committed, and came back out of git with
# 18,708 CRLFs.
#
# The .onnx files are already detected as binary today; naming them here means that does not
# depend on the heuristic happening to look at the right bytes.
src/OverTranslate/ocrmodels/**/dict.txt -text
*.onnx binary

# launcher.manifest is embedded byte for byte into the launcher's resource section, so its
# line endings decide the hash of a binary we ship. With core.autocrlf it checks out as CRLF
# on Windows (+25 bytes) and a rebuild no longer matches the binary committed under dist/,
# which silently breaks "build it yourself and compare the hash" — hit in practice.
# That binary's whole value is having bytes that never change across releases (#210).
src/OverTranslate.Launcher/launcher.manifest -text
